the solve rates for the Within, Cross, and even the basic SFT model were statistically indistinguishable. Even after increasing the evaluation budget to $k=8$k=8 attempts per task to gain more precision, the gap between Cross and Within remained a negligible +0.16 percentage points.\n\nWhile RL did improve performance on the \"source\" harnesses seen during training, these gains did not transfer to the unseen environment. This indicates that the learning occurring during multi-harness RL is primarily \"configuration adaptation\"—the agent is learning to better navigate the specific interfaces and quirks of Aider or OpenHands, rather than developing a more robust, generalized understanding of how to fix software bugs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents · Pith (https://pith.science/paper/2610.16523)\nciteturn82search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: yesterday; Interventions occur both intra-run (hot-swapped without restart) and inter-run (carried across training runs). ... The paper shows that co-evolving the user simulator, environment, and training configuration alongside the policy, mediated by a meta-agent, leads to substantial performance gains in multi-turn RL for user-centric agents. ...   * Meta-agent-guided adaptation could reduce the manual effort of debugging simulators, reward functions, and hyperparameters for multi-turn agent tasks.\nThe symbolic user simulator provides deterministic control over information disclosure, mixed with an LLM simulator via an epsilon-greedy strategy. Interventions occur both intra-run (hot-swapped without restart) and inter-run (carried across training runs).\n\nExtended reading notes\n\n### Core claim\n\nThe paper shows that co-evolving the user simulator, environment, and training configuration alongside the policy, mediated by a meta-agent, leads to substantial performance gains in mul